-- title:
--   Proof of Proposition 3.1, Measurability — u ↦ Ψᵃ_B(u)(t) is measurable on D[0,∞) for each t ≥ 0
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a distribution function on $\mathbb R$ with law $\mu$, and $a\in\mathbb R$. Equip $D[0,\infty)$ with the Kolmogorov σ-algebra generated by the evaluations $u\mapsto u(t)$, $t\ge0$. Then for every $t\ge0$ the map
--   $$u\longmapsto \Psi^a_B(u)(t)=\int_{[0,t]}(u(t-s)+a)^+\,dB(s)$$
--   from $D[0,\infty)$ to $\mathbb R$ is measurable.
--
--   The paper reduces the measurability of $\Psi^a_B:D[0,\infty)\to D[0,\infty)$ to exactly this statement; together with the previous milestone ($\Psi^a_B$ maps càdlàg paths to càdlàg paths) it is that measurability. It is the step from which the measurability of the regulator map $\varphi^a_B$ follows.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper states measurability for the Borel σ-field of the Skorohod $J_1$ topology and identifies it with the Kolmogorov σ-field (Billingsley 1999, Theorem 16.6); the Lean uses the Kolmogorov σ-field directly.
-- source:
--   Reed, The G/GI/N Queue in the Halfin–Whitt Regime, arXiv:0912.2837v1, p. 35, proof of Proposition 3.1 (Measurability)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ReedGGN_Regulator_PathSpace
import Definitions.Def_ReedGGN_Regulator_Equation

namespace ReedGGN.Regulator

open MeasureTheory

/-- Proof of Proposition 3.1, Measurability (pp. 34–35): for each `t ≥ 0`, the map
`u ↦ Ψ^a_B(u)(t)` is measurable from `D[0,∞)` with its Kolmogorov (cylinder) σ-algebra to
`ℝ`. Together with `psi_cadlag` this is the measurability of `Ψ^a_B : D[0,∞) → D[0,∞)`. -/
theorem psi_measurable (μ : Measure ℝ) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (a : ℝ) :
    ∀ t, 0 ≤ t → Measurable (fun u : Dpath => psi μ a u.path t) := by sorry

end ReedGGN.Regulator
